"ml-en\"><h3>📑 School slides — GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 (45 slides)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">matched by the chapter number in the deck name. File: GSF301_Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport_12Mar2026.pdf</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><h3>📑 Slide của trường — GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 (45 slide)</h3>\n<p class=\"ghi-chu\">ghép theo số chương ghi trong tên deck. Tệp: GSF301_Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport_12Mar2026.pdf</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/001.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 1: GSF301\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 1/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>GSF301<br>                                        SEA FREIGHT FORWARDING<br><br>                                                            Unit 2:<br><br>                                  International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   1</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/002.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 2: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 2/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>     Learning Objectives<br><br>           Upon successful completion of this unit, students will be able to:<br><br>          - Explain the fundamental reasons for the necessity of international governance<br>               and regulation in maritime transport.<br>          - Identify major international organizations in maritime industry.<br>          - Identify major international conventions in maritime industry.<br>          - Examine main international legal regimes governing the carrier's liability for the<br>               carriage of goods by sea (e.g.,Hague-Visby Rules, Hamburg Rules)<br><br>          - Identify other important rules and conventions affecting the carriage of goods for<br>               specific cargo types (e.g., IMDG Code), legal principles like General Average.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   2</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/003.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 3: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 3/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>                     NO                                  CONTENTS<br><br>                     2.1     Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br><br>                     2.2     Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>                     2.3     Other Important Principles, Conventions, and Regulations<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   3</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/004.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 4: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 4/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>       2.1 Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br>      2.1.1 The Role and Influence of International Organizations<br>      ❖ International organizations provide platforms for member states and industry stakeholders to<br>            discuss common issues, negotiate agreements, and establish unified standards that apply across<br>            national borders.<br><br>      ❖ Intergovernmental organizations, like those within the United Nations system, often have the<br>            mandate to develop international treaties and legally binding conventions.<br><br>      ❖ Non-governmental organizations, representing specific industry interests (shipowners, lawyers,<br>            etc.), play a vital role in contributing expertise, lobbying governments, and developing practical<br>            standards and contractual frameworks used in daily operations. Their influence collectively<br>            creates the complex governance structure that underpins safe and efficient global shipping.<br><br>      ❖ These organizations vary in their legal standing, from formal intergovernmental bodies<br>            established by international treaty (like IMO) to non-governmental associations representing<br>            commercial interests (like BIMCO or WSC). Their influence is derived from the willingness of<br>            member states and industry players to adopt and adhere to the standards they promote.<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                          ©VLI-FPTU   4</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/005.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 5: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 5/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>      2.1 Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br><br>      2.1.2 International Maritime Organization (IMO)<br>      ❖ IMO headquartered in London is the foremost international body responsible for maritime<br>             safety, security, and the prevention of pollution from ships. Established in 1948 and coming into<br>             force in 1958 as a specialized agency of the United Nations, the IMO provides a global<br>             regulatory framework for the shipping industry.<br>      ❖ Key focus areas: Maritime Safety, Maritime Security, Environmental Protection, Legal Matters;<br>             and Technical Co-operation.<br>      ❖ Key IMO Conventions: International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS); International<br>             Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships (MARPOL); International Convention on Standards<br>             of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Seafarers (STCW ) .<br><br>       (Reference: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/ListOfConventions.aspx )<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                               ©VLI-FPTU   5</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/006.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 6: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 6/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>     2.1 Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br>      2.1.3 United Nations Conference on Trade and Development (UNCTAD)<br>      ❖ UNCTAD was established in 1964 and headquartered in Geneva to promote inclusive and<br>            sustainable global trade and development. It serves as a UN body focused on helping<br>            developing countries integrate into the global economy. It collaborates with governments,<br>            businesses, and civil society to shape fairer trade systems. UNCTAD provides research, policy<br>            recommendations, and technical assistance on trade, investment, and development issues.<br>      ❖ While not exclusively focused on maritime transport, UNCTAD plays a significant role in this sector, and<br>            provides valuable economic analysis and data on maritime trade flows, fleet development, port<br>            performance, and freight rates, notably through its annual flagship publication, the Review of Maritime<br>            Transport.<br>      (Reference: https://unctad.org/ )<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   6</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/007.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 7: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 7/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>       2.1 Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br>       2.1.4 Baltic and International Maritime Council (BIMCO)<br>       ❖ BIMCO is the world's largest shipping association, representing shipowners, operators, brokers,<br>             and agents globally.<br>       ❖ Established in 1905, BIMCO is a non-governmental organization, and provides industry-leading<br>             contracts, guidelines, and regulatory advocacy for the maritime sector. It is known for<br>             developing standardized shipping agreements, such as charter parties and bills of lading, to<br>             streamline global trade.<br>       ❖ BIMCO members cover 62% of the world's tonnage and consist of local, global, small, and large<br>             companies. It has over 2,000 members in 130 countries (BIMCO, April 2025). By creating<br>             standardized contracts, BIMCO facilitates smoother and more efficient transactions in a<br>             complex global market, reducing disputes and providing legal certainty for contractual parties<br>             involved in sea transport.<br>       (Reference: https://www.bimco.org/about-bimco/ )<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   7</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/008.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 8: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 8/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>       2.1 Key International Organizations Governing Sea Transport<br><br>       2.1.5 Other Relevant International Bodies<br>       ❖ World Shipping Council (WSC): Established in 2000, WSC is an association representing the<br>             international liner shipping industry, primarily focusing on container shipping lines. It is a non-<br>             governmental organization. (https://www.worldshipping.org/)<br>       ❖ International Chamber of Shipping (ICS): Established in 1921, ICS is a global trade association for<br>             shipowners and operators, representing national shipowner associations. It is a non-<br>             governmental organization. (https://www.ics-shipping.org/ )<br>       ❖ Comité Maritime International (CMI): Established in 1897, CMI is a non-governmental not-for-<br>             profit international organization, the object of which is to contribute by all appropriate means<br>             and activities to the unification of maritime law in all its aspects. It is historically significant for<br>             its pioneering work in drafting many of the early international maritime law conventions.<br>             (https://comitemaritime.org/)<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   8</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/009.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 9: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 9/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>          2.2.1 Conventions Related to Vessel Safety and Environmental Protection<br>          2.2.1.1 Key aspects of SOLAS (Safety of Life at Sea): a cornerstone of maritime safety regulations.<br>          2.2.1.2 Key aspects of MARPOL (Prevention of Pollution from Ships) and IMO 2020 Sulphur Cap:<br>              MARPOL is the key convention preventing pollution from ships. It includes regulations aimed<br>              at preventing both accidental pollution (e.g., from collisions or groundings) and operational<br>              pollution (e.g., discharge of waste).<br>                IMO 2020 Sulphur Cap is a significant regulation under MARPOL Annex VI, which came into<br>                effect on 1 January 2020, mandating a reduction in the maximum sulphur content of fuel oil<br>                used by ships operating outside designated Emission Control Areas (ECAs) from 3.5% to 0.5% .<br>                This regulation implemented under MARPOL Annex VI is focused on preventing air pollution<br>                from ships, particularly emissions of sulphur oxides (SOx) and nitrogen oxides (NOx).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                            ©VLI-FPTU   9</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/010.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 10: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 10/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>     2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>      2.2.1 Conventions Related to Vessel Safety and Environmental Protection<br>      2.2.1.3 Verification of Container Mass (SOLAS VGM requirement): A specific and critical safety<br>          regulation introduced as an amendment to SOLAS Unit VI (Carriage of Cargoes), effective from 1<br>          July 2016. The requirement mandates that the Verified Gross Mass (VGM) of a packed container<br>          must be obtained and provided to the ship's master or his representative and the terminal<br>          operator prior to it being loaded onto a ship.<br>      There are two permitted methods for obtaining the VGM:<br>      ➢ Method 1: Weighing the packed container using calibrated and certified equipment; or<br>      ➢ Method 2: Weighing all packages and cargo items, including the mass of pallets, dunnage and<br>            other securing material to be packed in the container and adding the tare mass of the container<br>            to the sum of the single masses, using a certified method approved by the competent authority<br>            of the State in which packing of the container was completed.<br>      Method 2 will not be practical for shippers of bulk commodities like iron ore, grain, etc.<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                        ©VLI-FPTU   10</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/011.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 11: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 11/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>                 Verified Gross Mass Form<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                         ©VLI-FPTU   11</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/012.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 12: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 12/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2 Rules and Conventions Governing the Carriage of Goods by Sea<br>          2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>          The first major step towards harmonizing the law and balancing the interests of carriers and cargo<br>             owners was the International Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules of Law relating<br>             to Bills of Lading, adopted in Brussels in 1924, commonly known as the Hague Rules. Prior to<br>             this convention, contract terms were heavily dictated by carriers, often containing extensive<br>             exemption clauses that severely limited their liability, even for negligence.<br>          The Hague Rules 1924 were developed to address this imbalance. They represent a compromise between<br>              the interests of carriers (who faced inherent risks at sea and wanted protection) and cargo owners (who<br>              needed reliable transport and fair treatment).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   12</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/013.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 13: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 13/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>        2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>         2.2.2 Rules and Conventions Governing the Carriage of Goods by Sea<br>         2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>         With the advent of containerization and other significant changes, the traditional focus on<br>            breakbulk cargo and the outdated liability limits became increasingly problematic. The Visby<br>            Protocol of 1968 and the Brussels Protocol of 1979 introduced amendments aimed at<br>            modernization.<br>                   Highlighted: The International Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules of Law Relating to Bills of<br><br>                   Lading, signed in Brussels on 25 Aug 1924 (“Hague Rules”) and its additional Protocols of 23 Feb 1968<br><br>                   (“Visby Rules”) and 21 Dec 1979 (“SDR” Protocol). Effective 2 June 1931/ 23 June 1977/ 24 Feb 1982.<br>                   References:<br>                     -Hague Rules 1924 : http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/haguerules1924.html<br>                     -Visby Protocol 1968: http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/visbyrules1968.html<br>                     -SDR Protocol 1979: http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/sdrprotocol1979.html<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   13</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/014.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 14: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 14/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>        2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>           2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>           Main contents:<br>           ― Scopes of application (Article X, I)<br>           ― General obligations of carriers (Art II, III)<br>           ― General obligations of shippers (Art III)<br>           ― Bill of Lading, and burden of proof (Art III rule 3,7; Art IV rule 2)<br>           ― Applicability to charter parties ( Art I, V)<br>           ― Exceptions (Art IV rule 2) and limitation (Art IV rule 5) of liability for carriers)<br>           ― Delivery, and time bar of one year (Art IV, rule 6)<br>           ― Signature , ratification / accession, denunciation, amendment ( Art XI ~ XVI)<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   14</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/015.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 15: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 15/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>      2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>       2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br>       ❖ The Hague-Visby Rules do not apply to carriage of<br>                 o Live animals<br>                 o Cargo carried on deck and stated in the bill of lading as being<br>                      carried on deck.<br>       ❖ Period of Liability: From the time when the goods are loaded on to the<br>             time they are discharged from the ship.<br>       Hague-Visby Rules do not apply to periods of time when the cargo is<br>       carried by land or air under a through bill of lading or combined transport<br>       document.<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   15</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/016.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 16: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 16/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>       2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>        2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br>        ❖ Obligations for carriers:<br>        -Article III, Rule 1: “The carrier shall be bound before and at the beginning of the voyage<br>        to exercise due diligence to:<br>        (a) Make the ship seaworthy.<br>        (b) Properly man, equip and supply the ship.<br>        (c) Make the holds, refrigerating and cool chambers, and all other parts of the ship in which<br>        goods are carried, fit and safe for their reception, carriage and preservation.”<br>        -Article III, Rule 2: “the carrier shall properly and carefully load, handle, stow, carry, keep,<br>        care for, and discharge the goods carried.”<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                     ©VLI-FPTU   16</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/017.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 17: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 17/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>          2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br>          ❖ Obligations for carriers:<br>          Article III, Rule 3: After receiving the goods into his charge the carrier or the master or agent of<br>          the carrier shall, on demand of the shipper, issue to the shipper a bill of lading showing among<br>          other things:<br>          (a) The leading marks necessary for identification of the goods as the same are furnished in writing<br>          by the shipper before the loading of such goods starts, provided such marks are stamped or<br>          otherwise shown clearly upon the goods if uncovered, or on the cases or coverings in which such<br>          goods are contained, in such a manner as should ordinarily remain legible until the end of the<br>          voyage.<br>          (b) Either the number of packages or pieces, or the quantity, or weight, as the case may be, as<br>          furnished in writing by the shipper.<br>          (c) The apparent order and condition of the goods.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                            ©VLI-FPTU   17</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/018.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 18: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 18/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>         2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>         ❖ 17 Exceptions to liability (Article IV, Rule 2)<br>         (a) Act, neglect, or default of the master, mariner, pilot, or the servants of the carrier in the<br>         navigation or in the management of the ship.<br>         (b) Fire, unless caused by the actual fault or privity of the carrier.<br>         (c) Perils, dangers and accidents of the sea or other navigable waters.<br>         (d) Act of God.<br>         (e) Act of war.<br>         (f) Act of public enemies.<br>         (g) Arrest or restraint or princes, rulers or people, or seizure under legal process.<br>         (h) Quarantine restrictions.<br>         (i) Act or omission of the shipper or owner of the goods, his agent or representative.<br>         (j) Strikes or lockouts or stoppage or restraint of labour from whatever cause, whether partial or<br>         general.<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                          ©VLI-FPTU   18</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/019.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 19: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 19/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>     2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>     2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>     ❖ 17 Exceptions to liability (Article IV, Rule 2)<br>     (k) Riots and civil commotions.<br>     (l) Saving or attempting to save life or property at sea.<br>     (m) Wastage in bulk or weight or any other loss or damage arising from inherent defect, quality or<br>     vice of the goods.<br>     (n) Insufficiency of packing.<br>     (o) Insufficiency or inadequacy of marks.<br>     (p) Latent defects not discoverable by due diligence.<br>     (q) Any other cause arising without the actual fault or privity of the carrier, or without the actual<br>     fault or neglect of the agents or servants of the carrier, but the burden of proof shall be on the<br>     person claiming the benefit of this exception to show that neither the actual fault or privity of the<br>     carrier nor the fault or neglect of the agents or servants of the carrier contributed to the loss or<br>     damage.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   19</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/020.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 20: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 20/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>    2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>     2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>     ❖ Claims and Time bar<br>     • Notice of Loss or Damage: Before or upon discharge for apparent<br>       loss/damage; or 3 days maximum if loss or damage is not apparent.<br>     • Time bar: Any claim in respect of the goods carried will be time<br>       barred unless proceedings have been commenced within one year<br>       of their delivery, or the date when they should have been delivered.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                             ©VLI-FPTU   20</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/021.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 21: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 21/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>     2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>      2.2.2.1 The Hague Rules and the Hague-Visby Rules<br><br>      ❖ Limitation of liability<br>     Carriers can limit their liability for cargo damage of undisclosed cargo value.<br>     ⮚The Hague Rules 1924:              100 GBP/package or unit<br>            Art IV, rule 5: “Neither the carrier nor the ship shall in any event be or become liable for any loss or damage to or in connection with goods in an<br>            amount exceeding 100 pounds sterling per package or unit”<br>     ⮚The Hague-Visby Rules 1968: 10,000 francs/package or unit; or 30 francs/kilo of gross weight of<br>      the goods lost or damaged, whichever is the higher. A franc meant 65.5 milligrams of gold of<br>         millesimal fineness 900.<br>            Art IV, rule 5: “Unless the nature and value of such goods have been declared by the shipper before shipment and inserted in the bill of lading,<br>            neither the carrier nor the ship shall in any event be or become liable for any loss or damage to or in connection with the goods in an amount<br>            exceeding the equivalent of 10 000 francs per package or unit or 30 francs per kilo of gross weight of the goods lost or damaged, whichever is<br>            the higher.”<br>     ⮚The Hague-Visby “SDR” Rules 1979 : 666.67 SDR / package or unit; or 2 SDR / kilogram of gross<br>      weight of the goods lost or damaged, whichever is the higher. SDR (Special Drawing Right) as<br>      defined by IMF, the amounts shall be converted into national currency.<br>            Art IV, rule 5…<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                                                              ©VLI-FPTU   21</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/022.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 22: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 22/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>           2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>           Main contents:<br>          − Scope of application (part I, art 2)<br><br>          − Liability of the carrier ( part II)<br><br>          − Liability of the shipper (part III)<br><br>          − Transport document, Bill of Lading (part IV)<br><br>          − Claim and actions ( part V)<br><br>          − Supplementary provisions (part VI)<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   22</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/023.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 23: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 23/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>         ❖ Period of Responsibility<br><br>         Article 4. Period of responsibility<br><br>         1. The responsibility of the carrier for the goods under this Convention covers the period<br>             during which the carrier is in charge of the goods at the port of loading, during the<br><br>             carriage and at the port of discharge.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                ©VLI-FPTU   23</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/024.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 24: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 24/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>       2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>        2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>       ❖ Basis of liability<br>       • Loss<br><br>       • Damage<br><br>       • Delays in delivery: Delays in delivery occurs when the goods have not been delivered<br>           at the port of discharge within the time expressly agreed upon or, in the absence of such<br>           agreement, within the time which it would be reasonable to require of a diligent carrier,<br>           having regard to the circumstances of the case.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                   ©VLI-FPTU   24</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/025.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 25: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 25/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>           2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>            2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>           ❖ Exemptions:<br><br>           • No list of exemptions, however except in saving or attempting to save life or property at<br>               sea.( Art 5.6)<br><br>           • Live animals transport: the carrier is not liable for loss, damage or delay in delivery if he<br>               proves that he complied with any special instructions.<br><br>           • Fire due to objective reasons.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                       ©VLI-FPTU   25</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/026.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 26: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 26/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>         2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>         ❖ Limit of Liability<br>         Loss or damage:<br><br>               ✔ 835 SDR/ package or other shipping unit, or<br><br>               ✔ 2.5 SDR/ kilogram of gross weight whichever is the higher.<br><br>         If a container is used, the limits are calculated by reference to the number of packages or<br>         units stated in the bill of lading as packed in the container<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                  ©VLI-FPTU   26</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/027.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 27: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 27/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>        2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br><br>         ❖ Presumption of Fault<br><br>          The carrier is presumed to be liable for loss of or damage to the goods unless they can<br>         prove that they took all measures that could reasonably be required to avoid the<br>         occurrence and its consequences (Hamburg Rules, 1978, Art. 5(1)).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                              ©VLI-FPTU   27</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/028.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 28: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 28/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>          2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>          2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>         ❖ Limit of Liability<br>                Loss or damage:<br><br>                ✔835 SDR/ package or other shipping unit, or<br><br>                ✔2.5 SDR/ kilogram of gross weight whichever is the higher.<br><br>                If a container is used, the limits are calculated by reference to the number of<br>                packages or units stated in the bill of lading as packed in the container<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                             ©VLI-FPTU   28</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/029.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 29: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 29/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>        2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>        2.2.2.2 The Hamburg Rules<br>        ❖ Claims and Time Bar<br>          • Notice of Loss or Damage:<br>             ✔ Apparent loss or damage: 01 working days<br>             ✔ Not apparent: 15 consecutive days<br>             • Notice of Delays: 60 consecutive days after the day of delivery to the<br>                   consignee<br>             • Time Limit for Suit: within 02 years from delivery date or the date that<br>                   delivery should have taken place<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                           ©VLI-FPTU   29</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/030.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 30: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 30/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>          2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br>           2.2.2.3 The Rotterdam Rules<br>            Recognizing the ongoing fragmentation in the law governing the carriage of goods by sea due to<br>               the co-existence of the Hague, Hague-Visby, and Hamburg Rules, and acknowledging the<br>               increasing prevalence of door-to-door transport involving multiple modes, the United<br>               Nations Commission on International Trade Law (UNCITRAL) facilitated the drafting of a new<br>               convention: the United Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Carriage of<br>               Goods Wholly or Partly by Sea, adopted in Rotterdam in 2008. This convention, known as<br>               the Rotterdam Rules, aimed to provide a modern, comprehensive, and uniform legal<br>               framework for international contracts of carriage that include a sea leg.<br>           The Rotterdam Rules have not entered into force.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                        ©VLI-FPTU   30</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/031.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 31: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 31/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>          2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>           2.2.2.4 Other Important Rules and Conventions Related to Carriage Operations<br>           2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br><br>           The IMDG Code applies to:<br>           -     Cargo vessels transporting dangerous goods.<br>           -     Freight forwarders, port authorities, and shipping agents handling hazardous<br>                 cargo.<br>           -     Tankers, bulk carriers, and container ships that carry regulated substances.<br>           -     Crew members, emergency responders, and maritime safety professionals<br>                 involved in hazardous material transport.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   31</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/032.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 32: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 32/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>        2.2 Major International Conventions and Rules Governing Sea Transport<br><br>         2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br><br>         Contents, version 2016:<br>         ▪ Part 1: General provisions, definitions and training<br>         ▪ Part 2: Classification<br>         ▪ Part 3: Dangerous goods list, special provisions and exceptions<br>         ▪ Part 4: Packing and tank provisions<br>         ▪ Part 5: Consignment procedures<br>         ▪ Part 6: Construction and testing of packagings, intermediate bulk<br>            containers, large packagings, portable tanks, multiple-element gas<br>            containers and road tank vehicles<br>         ▪ Part 7: Provisions concerning transport operations<br>         ▪ Appendices and Index<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   32</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/033.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 33: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 33/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>         2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>                Labeling : 9 Classes<br>                Class 1: Explosives<br><br>                Class 2: Gases<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                              ©VLI-FPTU   33</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/034.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 34: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 34/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>       2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>        Labeling<br>        Class 3: Flammable liquids<br><br>        Class 4: Flammable solids<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                            ©VLI-FPTU   34</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/035.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 35: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 35/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>       2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>        Labeling<br>        Class 5: Oxidizing substances and organic peroxides<br><br>        Class 6: Toxic and infectious substances<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                            ©VLI-FPTU   35</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/036.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 36: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 36/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>         2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>          Labeling<br>          Class 7: Radioactive material<br><br>          Class 8: Corrosive substances<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                              ©VLI-FPTU   36</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/037.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 37: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 37/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>                 2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>         Labeling<br>         Class 9: Miscellaneous dangerous substances and articles<br><br>         * Marine pollutant mark<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                      ©VLI-FPTU   37</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/038.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 38: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 38/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>            2.2.2.4 a) The International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code<br>         Labeling<br><br>         Reference: IMDG Code, consolidated 2016.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   38</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/039.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 39: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 39/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>         2.3 Other Important Principles, Conventions, and Regulations<br><br>        2.3.1 General Average<br><br>        General Average is an ancient and fundamental principle of maritime law,<br>          predating many written statutes. Its origins can be traced back over 4000<br>          years and since merchandise has been carried by ships at sea. It formed part<br>          of the Rhodian Sea Law (possibly as early as the 3rd or 2nd century BC). In<br>          the Digest of the Emperor Justinian it referred to the Rhodian Sea Law and<br>          decreed that “if, in order to lighten a ship, merchandise is thrown overboard,<br>          that which has been given for all shall be replaced by the contribution of all”<br>          (Mitchelhill, 1990).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   39</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/040.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 40: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 40/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>         2.3 Other Important Principles, Conventions, and Regulations<br>          2.3.1 General Average<br>          Conditions for a Valid General Average Act:<br>          For a sacrifice or expenditure to be considered a General Average act, several strict conditions<br>              must typically be met, as defined by the York-Antwerp Rules. Key conditions include:<br>               ✓Imminent Peril: The ship and cargo must be in real and imminent danger.<br>               ✓Voluntary and Intentional: The sacrifice or expenditure must be made deliberately by the<br>                 master or authorized person, not as an unavoidable consequence of the peril.<br>               ✓For the Common Safety: The act must be intended to preserve the entire venture (ship,<br>                 cargo, freight), not just a specific part of it.<br>               ✓Reasonable: The sacrifice or expenditure must be reasonably necessary under the<br>                 circumstances.<br>               ✓Successful Preservation: The sacrifice or expenditure must be at least partly successful in<br>                 saving the venture from the peril.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   40</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/041.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 41: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 41/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>          2.3 Other Important Principles, Conventions, and Regulations<br><br>           2.3.2 Limitation of Liability for Maritime Claims (LLMC Convention)<br>           (further reading in the textbook Sea Transport)<br>           2.3.3 The Vietnam Maritime Code 2015<br>           Key Areas:<br>           - Sea-going Ships: Regulations on ship registration, ownership, and technical standards.<br>           - Seafarers &amp; Crew Members: Rights, responsibilities, and employment conditions.<br>           - Seaports &amp; Navigational Channels: Management, operation, and safety regulations.<br>           - Maritime Safety &amp; Environmental Protection: Measures to ensure safe navigation and<br>               pollution control.<br>           - Arrest of Ships: Legal procedures for detaining vessels in case of disputes.<br>           - Contracts for Carriage of Goods &amp; Passengers: Rules governing shipping agreements.<br>           - Marine Insurance: Coverage for maritime risks and liabilities.<br>           - Resolution of Maritime Disputes: Legal mechanisms for handling conflicts.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                       ©VLI-FPTU   41</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/042.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 42: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 42/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>     Revision<br>     1. A “Marine Pollutant mark” under IMDG is MOST associated with:<br>            a. Class 1 only<br>            b. Class 7 only<br>            c. Environmentally hazardous substances requiring special marking<br>            d. All non-hazardous cargo<br>            e. Container VGM forms<br>     2. SOLAS VGM requirement primarily aims to:<br>            a. Reduce customs duties<br>            b. Prevent stowage accidents from incorrect container weights<br>            c. Guarantee faster delivery<br>            d. Replace the bill of lading<br>            e. Abolish container seals<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                 ©VLI-FPTU   42</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/043.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 43: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 43/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>     Revision<br>     3. A shipper asks the freight forwarder to declare dangerous goods as general cargo to avoid additional<br>        IMDG charges. The shipment is urgent. What is the MOST appropriate action for the forwarder?<br>            a. Refuse and explain legal and safety consequences<br>            b. Accept the request to maintain client relationship<br>            c. Declare as general cargo and inform carrier later<br>            d. Ignore IMDG if cargo is well packed<br>            e. Ask warehouse to change cargo description<br>     4. A carrier attempts to limit liability below the amount provided under international conventions. How<br>        should this situation be evaluated?<br>            a. Accept lower liability to reduce dispute<br>            b. Compare limitation against applicable convention provisions<br>            c. Ignore convention terms<br>            d. Transfer responsibility to port authority<br>            e. Increase freight rate<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                      ©VLI-FPTU   43</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/044.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 44: UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 44/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 2: INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br>     Lesson summary<br>     • The international framework regulating sea transport is introduced in this unit. It explains key<br>       organizations such as IMO, UNCTAD, and BIMCO, which help develop global standards for maritime<br>       safety, environmental protection, and shipping operations.<br>     • It also discusses major international conventions such as SOLAS and MARPOL, as well as legal rules<br>       governing the carriage of goods by sea, including the Hague-Visby Rules and the Hamburg Rules.<br>     • In addition, it highlights important regulations and principles such as Verified Gross Mass (VGM)<br>       requirements, the IMDG Code for dangerous goods transport, and the concept of General Average, all<br>       of which contribute to maintaining safety, fairness, and efficiency in international shipping.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                   ©VLI-FPTU   44</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d02/045.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 45: END OF UNIT 2:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 2 International Organizations and Regulations in Sea Transport 12Mar2026 · slide 45/45</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>END OF UNIT 2:<br>                                  INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS AND<br>                                   REGULATIONS IN SEA TRANSPORT<br><br>                                  THANK FOR YOUR ATTENTION!<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                     ©VLI-FPTU   45</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><h3>🔗 Links on these slides — watch/read them</h3></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><h3>🔗 Link trong slide — xem/đọc thêm</h3></div>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/ListOfConventions.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/ListOfConventions.aspx</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://unctad.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://unctad.org/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bimco.org/about-bimco/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.bimco.org/about-bimco/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.worldshipping.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.worldshipping.org/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ics-shipping.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.ics-shipping.org/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://comitemaritime.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://comitemaritime.org/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/haguerules1924.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/haguerules1924.html</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/visbyrules1968.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/visbyrules1968.html</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/sdrprotocol1979.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">http://www.admiraltylawguide.com/conven/sdrprotocol1979.html</a></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Detailed lecture and 🎬 video for this session: coming later. For now follow the slides, the materials and the tasks above.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Bài giảng chi tiết và 🎬 video cho buổi này: sẽ bổ sung sau. Trước mắt học theo slide, tài liệu và nhiệm vụ ở trên.</div></div>"
