//media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/026.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 26: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 26/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>                   3.5.2 Cargo Handling Equipment<br>                     Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs) and Automated Stacking Cranes (ASCs): Increasingly used in<br>                     automated terminals for efficient, autonomous horizontal transport and stacking.<br><br>                  Automated Guided Vehicle (AGV) in Container           Automated Stacking Crane (ASC). Photo: Kuenz<br>                  Terminal Altenwerder. Photo: HHLA/<br>                  www.porttechnology.org<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                                        ©VLI-FPTU   26</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/027.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 27: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 27/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>                   3.5.2 Cargo Handling Equipment<br><br>                                  Example of Equipment performance:<br><br>                                  • Quayside Container Gantry Crane<br>                                          22 moves / hour net (18 gross)<br>                                  • Mobile Harbour Crane<br>                                          18-20 moves / hour net (15-17 gross)<br>                                  • Ship’s Crane<br>                                          10 moves / hour net (7 gross)<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                   ©VLI-FPTU   27</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/028.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 28: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 28/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>                  3.5.3 Storage Facilities<br>                   Areas and structures within the port for temporarily storing cargo before it is moved<br>                  inland or loaded onto a vessel.<br>                         o   Container Yards: Large paved areas for stacking containers. Can be served by<br>                             cranes or straddle carriers.<br>                         o   Warehouses and Sheds: For storing bagged, baled, crated, or other general<br>                             cargo, especially breakbulk.<br>                         o   Silos: For storing dry bulk cargoes like grain or cement.<br>                         o   Tanks: For storing liquid bulk cargoes like oil, chemicals, or vegetable oils.<br>                         o   Open Storage Areas: For cargo not requiring covered storage (e.g., vehicles,<br>                             steel products, logs).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                               ©VLI-FPTU   28</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/029.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 29: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 29/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>            3.5.4 Hinterland Connections<br><br>            The transportation links connecting the port to its inland service area, so called<br>            hinterland. Efficient hinterland connections are crucial for preventing port<br>            congestion and ensuring smooth cargo flow. A port’s hinterland is its inland<br>            market area connected via transportation networks, crucial for cargo flows and<br>            competitiveness. Hinterlands can be competitive or non-competitive, shaped by<br>            transport infrastructure and economic geography. Efficient hinterland<br>            connectivity through multimodal corridors is essential for port growth and<br>            integration into global supply chains. It plays a critical role in port<br>            competitiveness and regional trade dynamics (Ma, 2020).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                            ©VLI-FPTU   29</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/030.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 30: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 30/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>          3.5.4 Hinterland Connections<br><br>                 o   Road Connections: Highways and roads connecting the port to distribution<br>                     centers and industrial areas. Requires sufficient capacity and efficient gate<br>                     procedures at the port.<br>                 o   Rail Connections: Rail lines connecting the port's terminals to inland rail<br>                     networks, often used for transporting large volumes of containers<br>                     (intermodal trains) or bulk cargo over long distances.<br>                 o   Inland Waterways: Rivers and canals providing barge transport connections<br>                     to inland locations, particularly important in regions with extensive river<br>                     networks.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                ©VLI-FPTU   30</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/031.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 31: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 31/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>             3.6 Feeder Services Between Ports<br><br>             A feeder service uses smaller container vessels (feeder vessels) to<br>             transport containers between a major transshipment hub port (a large<br>             port where main line vessels call) and surrounding smaller &quot;spoke&quot;<br>             ports that are not regularly visited by the larger main line vessels.<br>             Feeder services are recognized in maritime economics as critical<br>             components of hub-and-spoke networks, facilitating cargo flows<br>             between major hubs and regional ports (Stopford, 1997; Ma, 2020).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                   ©VLI-FPTU   31</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/032.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 32: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 32/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>          3.7 Port Functions and Services<br><br>          1) Services to Ships:<br>          ➢  Sea/Waterway-side services: These include essential navigational and safety services such<br>             as dredging to maintain channel depth, pilotage to guide ships safely, mooring and unmooring<br>             operations to secure vessels, and vessel repair and maintenance.<br>          ➢  Ship/Shore interface services: These involve berthing (assigning and managing berth space),<br>             bunkering (fuel supply), provisioning, and other services necessary for vessel turnaround.<br>          2) Services to Cargo:<br>          ➢  Ship/shore interface cargo services: Handling activities such as loading, unloading, stowing,<br>             and securing cargo on vessels. Efficient cargo handling is crucial for minimizing vessel<br>             turnaround time and ensuring cargo integrity.<br>          ➢  Landside cargo services: These include consolidation, storage, warehousing, packaging,<br>             customs clearance, and distribution functions performed in the port’s hinterland or terminal<br>             areas. Ports increasingly add value by offering logistics and supply chain services beyond basic<br>             cargo handling.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                           ©VLI-FPTU   32</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/033.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 33: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 33/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>           3.8 Port Automation<br><br>           A defining characteristic of modern terminal operations, especially for container<br>           terminals, is the high degree of automation. Automated container terminals<br>           provide significant advantages in saving terminal labour costs, improving port<br>           capacity, reducing the energy consumption of port equipment, and enhancing<br>           the image of ports (UNCTAD, 2018 &amp; 2022).<br><br>           An automated container terminal can increase productivity by 10- 35%, reduce<br>           the number of workers by over 45%, and save operating costs by 25-55%, and<br>           reduce carbon emissions by over 50% (McKinsey, 2018).<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                           ©VLI-FPTU   33</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/034.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 34: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 34/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>        3.9 Challenges and Developments in Ports<br>        Ports face constant pressure to adapt and improve in response to<br>        technological advancements, environmental concerns, security threats, and<br>        the ever-increasing demands of global trade.<br>           1.   Adapting to Vessel Upsizing<br>           2.   Congestion<br>           3.   Automation and Digitalization<br>           4.   Environmental Challenges<br>           5.   Security<br>           6.   Hinterland Connectivity and Performance<br>           7.   Resilience (vulnerability of ports and their surrounding logistics networks to natural disasters,<br>                pandemics, as seen with COVID-19 impacts on port operations and labor)<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                              ©VLI-FPTU   34</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/035.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 35: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 35/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br>     Revision<br>     1. If cargo damage is discovered after discharge, what should be done FIRST under convention<br>        procedures?<br>            a. Ignore minor damage<br>            b. Cancel insurance<br>            c. Issue notice of claim within required time<br>            d. Replace carrier<br>            e. Amend invoice<br>     2. A shipment of vehicles needs sea transport. Which vessel type is MOST suitable?<br>            a. Bulk carrier<br>            b. Oil tanker<br>            c. Fishing vessel<br>            d. Tugboat<br>            e. Ro-Ro vessel<br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                            ©VLI-FPTU   35</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/036.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 36: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 36/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br>     Revision<br>     3. Turning basin and channel width primarily affect:<br>            a. Customs duty rate<br>            b. Maneuverability and two-way traffic feasibility for large vessels<br>            c. Container ISO code format<br>            d. Cargo commodity classification<br>            e. Insurance underwriting limits<br>     4. A feeder service is MOST accurately:<br>            a. A long-haul service between major continents<br>            b. A trucking shuttle inside a terminal<br>            c. A rail service to inland depots only<br>            d. A bunker supply operation<br>            e. A small vessel service linking hub ports with spoke ports not called by mainline ships<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                         ©VLI-FPTU   36</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/037.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 37: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 37/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br>     Revision<br>     5. A carrier considers replacing older vessels with eco-friendly vessels. What is the MOST appropriate<br>        evaluation criterion?<br>            a. Vessel color<br>            b. Environmental regulation compliance and long-term cost savings<br>            c. Crew uniform<br>            d. Dock location<br>            e. Port restaurant quality<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                     ©VLI-FPTU   37</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/038.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 38: UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 38/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>UNIT 3: GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br>     Lesson summary<br>     • This unit introduces the geography of seaports, global shipping routes, and port facilities, highlighting<br>       their roles in international maritime transport and global supply chains.<br>     • It first examines major global trade routes and maritime chokepoints, which connect key economic<br>       regions and facilitate international trade. It also discusses major world seaports and Vietnam’s seaport<br>       system, emphasizing their capacity, cargo throughput, and importance in regional logistics networks.<br>     • In addition, the unit explains port accessibility and infrastructure, including physical constraints,<br>       environmental factors, and navigational support required for safe vessel operations. It also introduces<br>       port facilities and cargo handling equipment, such as cranes, container yards, storage facilities, and<br>       hinterland transport connections.<br>     • Finally, it discusses feeder services, port functions, automation, and current challenges faced by ports,<br>       including congestion, vessel upsizing, environmental concerns, and the need for improved connectivity<br>       and technological development in modern port operations.<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                                                                          ©VLI-FPTU   38</p></div>\n<div class=\"anh-slide\"><img src=\"https://media.cuongthai.com/images/academy/GSF301/flm1/d03/039.webp\" alt=\"GSF301 · GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 39: END OF UNIT 3:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /><p class=\"chu-thich\">GSF301 Unit 3 Geography of Seaport, Shipping Routes, and Port Facilities 12Mar2026 · slide 39/39</p></div>\n<div class=\"trich-slide\"><p class=\"nhan\">Trên slide · On the slide</p><p>END OF UNIT 3:<br>      GEOGRAPHY OF SEA PORT, SHIPPING ROUTES, AND PORT FACILITIES<br><br>                                  THANK FOR YOUR ATTENTION!<br><br>GSF301 - Sea Freight Forwarding                               ©VLI-FPTU   39</p></div>\n<div class=\"ml-en\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Detailed lecture and 🎬 video for this session: coming later. For now follow the slides, the materials and the tasks above.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ml-vi\"><div class=\"note-ct\">📝 Bài giảng chi tiết và 🎬 video cho buổi này: sẽ bổ sung sau. Trước mắt học theo slide, tài liệu và nhiệm vụ ở trên.</div></div>"
